"Many of u us them several times a dy without really noticig.And yet the way we in lifts,or elevaors as they are known in the US,reveals a hidden anxiety. Passengers seem to know instinctively how to arrange themselves in an levator,like the dots on a die.With each additional passenger, the bodies shift,going into the open spces. On your own,you ca do whteve you want-it's yor own little box.If there are two of you,you take different corners, creating the greatest distance. When a third person enters, yu wl unconsciously fom a triangle .nd whe there is a square, with someone I every corner.A fifth person is probable oing to have to stand in the middle. Why are we so awkward in lifts? \"You don't have enough space,\" ays Professor Babette Reneerg, a clinical psychologist at the Free University of Berlin.\"Usually when we meet other people w have about an arm's length of distance between us. And thats not possible in most eleators, so it's a ry unusual setting It's unnatural.\" But pehaps there is more to it than just social awkwardness. \"In the back of or minds we are  lttle anxious,\" ays Nick White,an office in New York who was unfortunae enogh to be trapped in a lift for41 hours. \"e don't like to b locked into a place.We want to get out f the elevatr a soo as possible, you know, it's a frightening place to be.\" During his terrble experience, he began to think of anther encloses space-a tomb. Dr.Lee Gra arees tht  sense of disempowerment  the main cause of ie anxiety. \"You're in a machie that's moving, over which you have no control. You cannot see the elevator engine, yo don' know how it's working,\" he sas."
