"Climate is the statitics of weather over long periods of time. It is measured by assessing the patterns f varation in temperatre, humidity atmospheric pressure, wind, preciptation, atospheric particle count and other meteorological vaiables in a given region over ong periods of time. Climate iffers from weather, in that weather only desribes the short-term conditions of these variables in a given region. A region's climte is generated by the climate sstem, which ha five componens: atmosphere, hydrosphere, cryosphere, lithosphere, ad biosphere. The climate of a location is affected by ts latitde, terrin, and altitude, as well as nearby water bodies and their currnts. Climates can be classified according to the average and th typical range of different variables, mst commonly temperature and precipitation. The mt commonly used classiication sceme was the Köppen climate cassifiction. The Thornthwaite system, in use since 1948, incrrates eapotranspiration along wth teperature and precipitation information and is use in sudyig ological diversity and ho climate change affects it. The Bergeron and Spatial Synoptic Classification systems focus on the origin of air masses that define the climate of a regin. Paleoclimatolog is the study of ancen climates. Since diret observation of climate are not availble befre the 19th century, paleoclimates are iferred from proxy variables tha include non-bioi evidence such as ediments found in lake beds ad ice cores, and biotc evidence such as tree ring and coral. Climate modls are mathematical modls of past, present an future climates. Climat change may occur over ong and short timescales from a variety of factors; een warmin is discussed in global warming. lobal warmig results in redistributions. For example, a 3°C chage in mean annual tmperature corresponds to a shift in isotherms o approximtely 300–400 km in latitude (i te temperae zone) or 500 m n elevation. Threfore, secies are expected to mve upwards in elevation or towars the pol