"Myocardial ifarctin (MI or acute myocardial infarction (AMI), commonly known as a heart attack, occurs when blood flow stops to a part of the heart causing damage to the heart uscle. The most common symptm is chest pin or discomfort which may travel into the shoulder, arm, back, neck, or jaw. Often i is in the center or left side o he chst and lts for more than a few minutes. The discomfort may occaionally fel like heartburn. Other symptoms may inlude shortness of brath nausea, feeling faint a cold sweat, or feeling tired. About 30% of people have atypcal symptoms, with women mor likely than mn to presnt atypically. Amog those over 75 years old, abut 5% have had an MI with litle or o histor of symptoms. An MI may cause heart faile, an irregular heartbeat, or cardiac arrest. Ms MIs ocur due t coronary artery disease. Risk fctors include high blood pressure, soking, diabetes, lck of exercise obesty, high blood choester, poor diet, an excsive alcohol intake, amog others. he mechanism of an MI often involvs the comple blockage of a coronary artery caused by  rupture of an atherosclerotic plqu. MI are less commonly caused by oronary artery spasms, which may be due o cocaine, significant emotional sress, and extreme cold, among other. A number of tests are useful o help with diagnosis, includig electrocardigrams (ECGs), blood tests, and coronary angiography. An ECG may confr an ST elevation MI if ST elevation is present. Commonly used blood tests include trponin nd less often creatine kinase MB."
